# KNOWN GAP — BOTH BACKENDS. A user function whose name happens to match a libc
# symbol fails to compile with a raw C error, because user names reach the
# emitted C UNMANGLED.
#
#     int abs(int x):          ->  error: conflicting types for 'abs'
#         ...
#
# Measured 2026-08-24, one probe per name, all identical `conflicting types`:
#     abs · div · remove · exit · free · read · write · index   COLLIDE
#     open                                                      ok
#
# so it is not a curated keyword list, it is "whatever libc happens to declare"
# -- which means the set is PLATFORM-DEPENDENT and silently different on macOS,
# musl, or a newer glibc. A program that compiles for one user fails for another.
#
# ⚠ This is a LAYERING defect, not a naming policy question. The C-emit boundary
# is the one place the runtime symbol name IS the contract; user-defined names
# have no business sharing that namespace unmangled. Structs already mangle
# (`__gg_Point`), so the mechanism exists and is simply not applied to functions.
#
# ⚠ The diagnostic is the other half of the defect: the user wrote Gorget and
# gets a C compiler error naming a C symbol, with no indication that renaming
# their function is the fix.
#
# INTENDED (asserted below): compiles and prints 7. `abs` is an ordinary
# identifier in a language with no `abs` builtin; nothing in the Gorget spec
# reserves it. A teaching rejection at check time would be an acceptable
# alternative -- accept-then-fail-in-cc is not.
#
# Found 2026-08-24 by the robustness-map expansion; orchestrator-verified.

int abs(int x):
    if x < 0:
        return 0 - x
    return x

void main():
    print(abs(0 - 7))
